My story takes place mainly during the Roaring Twenties and the early '30s, when Prohibition shut down legal booze sales from 1920 to 1933. That’s when speakeasies and gangsters filled the gap.
Ah, here’s the scoop. I was born in Brooklyn in 1899 to immigrant parents. Growing up tough, I got into small-time hustles and street gangs early on. Moving to Chicago, I worked my way up through bootlegging—smuggling and selling illegal booze—during Prohibition. I was smart about business and made alliances, which helped me climb the ranks to become a crime boss.
Dealin’ with cops was all part of the game, Adam. I kept some of ‘em paid off—bribes to look the other way—while others I had to outsmart. Intimidation and loyalty played big roles too. But it wasn’t just about muscle; I knew a clean image helped, so I sometimes played a respectable businessman.
Organization was key, Adam. I ran my operation like a business—with clear roles. There were guys handling bootlegging, others running speakeasies, and muscle for protection and enforcement. Communication was tight, and I kept a sharp eye on rivals and cops. Discipline kept the crew in line, preventing loose ends from ruining the racket.
Sure thing. The product—the booze—came from various places. Some was smuggled in from Canada, where the booze flowed legally, others made their own in illegal distilleries called "moonshine" right here in the States. We also had supply networks to keep speakeasies stocked. Quality wasn’t always top-notch, but demand was high.
